So because when I built my hash table, I look at every element, find the slot, put it in

236
00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.300
there, takes constant time.

237
00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:29.360
If I say it takes constant time, if I'm pedantic, I should modify my hash table.

238
00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:36.660
Of course, it's likely to look like this.

239
00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.060
It depends on how you implement it, of course, but most likely it will look like this.

240
00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:44.000
But it doesn't matter, so we can read the hash table in constant time.

241
00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:49.600
And then when we probe the hash table,

242
00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:50.780
so maybe we read the other side,

243
00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:52.780
for each element we compute a hash value,

244
00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:53.600
takes constant time.

245
00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:57.500
We go to an element in the hash table,

246
00:16:57.520 --> 00:16:58.340
takes constant time.

247
00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:00.200
Then we check if it's equal,

248
00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:01.880
and now if this takes constant time or not,

249
00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:04.980
depends on how much faith you have in statistics

250
00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:06.380
and how good your hash function is.

251
00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.460
And so if you have a good hash function

252
00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:10.660
and you don't have a lot of collisions,

253
00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:12.960
hopefully no collisions or very few collisions,

254
00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.080
it takes constant time, then we get end up

255
00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:17.060
with linear runtime.

256
00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:21.760
Okay, so, hash tables are usually better than sorting

257
00:17:22.580 --> 00:17:23.840
if the data fits in memory.

258
00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:27.840
Okay, so then we can get nice linear access time.

259
00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:31.720
And so these are just two options.

260
00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.880
Of course, there are many, many, many other ways.

261
00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.160
You could use some other data structures

262
00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:38.700
to search it instead of sorting.

263
00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.500
We could use search trees,

264
00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:45.860
So red-black trees also, usually you don't want to do this by the way.

265
00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:51.720
Red-black trees are pretty slow, but we could, and some other mechanisms.

266
00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:52.900
So we have many choices here.

267
00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:57.700
And of course we will look at some details later in the lecture when we come to index

268
00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:58.040
structures.

269
00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:03.840
So this is already mentioned, so we could sort both lists and intersect, or build a

270
00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:08.040
hash table, or build index structure, or many other alternatives.

271
00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:11.260
Now note what is not an option.

272
00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:17.720
So I will now write down some code that you will hopefully not use.

273
00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:19.860
I just include it for completeness.

274
00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:27.540
So if I would have asked an arbitrary programmer to say, please you have two lists, find all

275
00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:32.020
entries that are current in both lists, you could be tempted to do the following.

276
00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:52.680
I would say I do a for loop for x in L1, for y in L2, if x equals y is x.

277
00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:55.040
So you could be tempted to do this.

278
00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:56.680
Don't do this.

279
00:18:56.840 --> 00:18:58.240
So don't do this.

280
00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:02.360
even though it's just four lines of code, it of course has terrible runtime.

281
00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:12.420
Because the runtime of this year is in the order of size of L1 times size of L2,

282
00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:18.420
and this means, asymptotically, we have this word here, we have an n-square runtime.

283
00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:20.940
where n is the size of the smaller list.

284
00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:27.640
So, n is the list size,

285
00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:30.620
And then we get n2 runtime.

286
00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:31.780
Don't do this.

287
00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:37.500
So n2 algorithms are usually a different system.

288
00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:42.440
So we cannot use n2 algorithms.

289
00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:48.180
So our algorithm have to be better than n2 usually.

290
00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:50.920
Now why is this the case?

291
00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:54.360
Because our n tends to be huge.

292
00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:56.500
So if you have a database system,

293
00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:01.840
then your n tends to be in the order of a few million,

294
00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.540
perhaps even many million.

295
00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:10.000
And clearly, if you say n is 1 million,

296
00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:13.240
so then n squared is a really unpleasant number.

297
00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:18.040
Because this here is 10 to the power of 6,

298
00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.960
and then n squared here is 10 to the power of 12.

299
00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:22.960
and 10 to the power of 12 is definitely a number

300
00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:24.660
that you don't want to see in your execution time.

301
00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.500
So, therefore, don't do this.

302
00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:30.420
n square is not good.

303
00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.560
As a rough rule of thumb, you can say,

304
00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:36.420
in a database system, you only want to use algorithms

305
00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.280
that have a runtime of n log n or better.

306
00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:44.160
Now, admittedly, it's not always possible to get this,

307
00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:46.520
but we want that everything that we do

308
00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:48.020
is n log n or faster,

309
00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:50.980
just because our n is so incredibly huge.

310
00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:52.820
And if you have a huge n,

311
00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:54.720
you cannot afford to do an n-square algorithm.

312
00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:58.680
So this is one of the challenges

313
00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.660
that we have when building a large scalable system.

314
00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:07.520
Okay, now, again, I said the problem is,

315
00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:09.300
simply if the data fits in memory,

316
00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:11.580
what do we do if the data does not fit in memory?

317
00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:14.220
So it could be that we have so many elements

318
00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:17.140
in my database system that I cannot store all of them.

319
00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:20.120
So what should we do?

320
00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:23.340
Now there are good and not so good cases.

321
00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:27.060
So the good case is, let's assume, remember we have two lists.

322
00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:31.640
Let's assume one of the two lists does fit in memory.

323
00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:33.780
Then everything is still okay.

324
00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:40.280
So if one fits, let's say L1 is a smaller one, if L1 fits in memory but L2 does not,

325
00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:45.760
that's okay, because the only thing I have to do is I take my L1, again I build a large

326
00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:50.060
hash table here. So this is the hash table for L1.

327
00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:53.840
So hash table for L1.

328
00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:57.960
And then I take my L2. Now this L2 is huge,

329
00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:00.400
but this doesn't matter. So even if it's really, really huge.

330
00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:06.140
L1 I can't load in my memory. Then I read a part of L2 that fits

331
00:22:06.140 --> 00:22:10.040
for example this one here. Then I probe the part that I've read

332
00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.520
from here to my hash table and find all the matches.

333
00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.080
Then I forget the part of L2 that I've read.

334
00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:17.140
I read the next part.

335
00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:20.500
Again, perform a lookup, find all the matches and so on.

336
00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:24.040
So we just process L2 piece by piece

337
00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:25.780
and we keep L1 in memory all the time.

338
00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:30.400
This is nearly as simple as in memory case.

339
00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:32.620
It's slightly more complicated, but it's not much.

340
00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:33.540
So that's okay.

341
00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.520
L2 just loads a smaller list into memory,

342
00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:38.960
build some index structure or hash table or something.

343
00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:41.680
We scan the large list and then we find all matches.

344
00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:44.880
Really no big deal.

345
00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:49.500
Now, of course, you probably see what's coming now.

346
00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:52.640
Yeah, so the interesting case, of course,

347
00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:56.740
is what happens if both lists are so large

348
00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:58.260
that they do not fit in memory.

349
00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:00.700
So we have a really, really large input.

350
00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:02.880
If you wanted to intersect two lists

351
00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:05.480
that are simply too large for my memory.

352
00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.140
So for example, it could be that I have a memory

353
00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:13.240
of 64 gigabytes, but my input is a terabyte.

354
00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:19.920
So then I cannot keep it, so I must do something to still find the matches.

355
00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:25.680
Now we come to external memory algorithms, and these tend to be much more nasty and difficult

356
00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.260
than the in-memory case.

357
00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:34.080
So first thing that you see is no direct interaction is possible.

358
00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:39.560
So it's simply not tractable to compare elements from one list to the other, like in a loop

359
00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:40.500
or anything like this.

360
00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:42.260
This is infeasible.

361
00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:46.500
Because accessing something from external memory,

362
00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:48.900
so worst case, a rotating disk,

363
00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:50.940
but even if it's not a rotating disk,

364
00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:52.940
but an SSD, which is much faster,

365
00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:54.620
even then accessing the SSD,

366
00:23:54.860 --> 00:23:57.020
it's much, much, much, much slower than memory.

367
00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:00.980
So we cannot just go to the disk and read something.

368
00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:03.000
This is not too expensive.

369
00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:03.940
We cannot do this.

370
00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.400
Now, what we could do,

371
00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:09.340
we could use sorting.

372
00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:12.380
We could take the two lists,

373
00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:14.220
then sort the two lists,

374
00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:15.900
and then the intersection.

375
00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:17.540
Because after sorting,

376
00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:18.480
the problem is easy.

377
00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:20.040
Remember how the intersection

378
00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:21.300
of two sorted lists worked.

379
00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:23.940
So we just take two by two sorted lists here.

380
00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:28.400
And I start reading in the beginning,

381
00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.720
and I always skip the smallest element

382
00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.480
until I have a match.

383
00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:35.940
So this is the strategy, basically.

384
00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:38.420
And this works, of course, beautifully,

385
00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:41.040
lists are too large.

386
00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:43.160
Because I just read the next element

387
00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:45.120
and of course I have to go to disk

388
00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:47.100
from time to time to fetch the next

389
00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:49.140
block of data from

390
00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:51.100
disk, but that's okay, because in memory

391
00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:52.520
I only have to keep one element.

392
00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:54.680
So I can easily keep this

393
00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:56.740
and I can do this kind of processing for

394
00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:58.880
arbitrary large lists. This works beautifully.

395
00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:00.980
The main problem here

396
00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:01.760
is sorting.

397
00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:06.880
How do I sort

398
00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:08.240
a list that does not fit in memory?

399
00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:11.920
cover this as a lecture, so you might know this,

400
00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:16.080
or if you encountered in a lecture on algorithms

401
00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.000
and data structures, you might have heard about

402
00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.200
n-way sorting, for example, this is one technique

403
00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:21.820
to do this.

404
00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:24.680
There are algorithms to sort data that is larger

405
00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:25.200
in memory.

406
00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:28.620
For now we ignore this, we're just saying, okay,

407
00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:30.840
this is one approach, but this is difficult.

408
00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:34.840
Or the alternative is, you use a kind of partitioning

409
00:25:34.840 --> 00:25:36.980
scheme, which means you break your problem

410
00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:40.720
into smaller problems until the smaller problems fit in memory.

411
00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:49.540
And this is usually an attractive solution, but this is again much more difficult than what we

412
00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:57.920
did before. So for example, what we can do is, I take here, this is my, again, I have a very large

413
00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:05.600
input here, this is a very large input, and now I read my input. For example, this I can fit in

414
00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:12.160
the parts that I can fit in memory, and then I partition it, which means I split this into

415
00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:15.680
multiple groups.

416
00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:21.300
For example, I say every value between 0 and 100 comes here, this bucket.

417
00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:28.400
Then everything between 100 and 1000 or so comes here, and so on.

418
00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:34.480
So I have many, many buckets, and I organize all my elements in buckets.

419
00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:39.920
So I do this for the first chunk, then I do this for the next chunk, again, distributing

420
00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:43.360
it in different buckets and so on.

421
00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:44.440
I do this for all my input.

422
00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:52.320
And afterwards I know that the first bucket contains always between 0 and 100, the second

423
00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:54.000
one between 100 and 1000 and so on.

424
00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:58.180
And I did this hopefully in a way such that each bucket fits in my memory.

425
00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:03.740
So that I can keep it in my memory.

426
00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:06.380
If it does not fit, I must use more buckets.

427
00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:08.740
So I just must use more partitions.

428
00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:11.660
Use as many partitions as I need

429
00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:13.280
until the result fits in memory.

430
00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:15.900
And once I have done this,

431
00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:18.180
then we come back to the example we had before,

432
00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:20.920
namely data fits in memory.

433
00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.320
Now again, we have two choices.

434
00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:25.420
Either we partition both sides,

435
00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:29.420
so then we can fall back to the real in-memory case,

436
00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:31.360
or we just partition one side,

437
00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:33.080
then we come back to the case

438
00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:41.200
advantage is we need less partitioning, disadvantage is we read the other side

439
00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:45.780
multiple times, also not super attractive. Usually we want to partition both sides.

440
00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:53.720
I just sketched a solution here, we will look at more examples how this

441
00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:58.280
recursive partitioning works in the lecture when we come to external memory

442
00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:02.900
hashing because, sorry, external memory joints because this is exactly what's the

443
00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:07.240
And I'm just saying this is already much, much more complex.

444
00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:10.100
So we must read my input, I must partition it,

445
00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.380
I must make sure my partitions are not too large,

446
00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:14.620
and all kinds of special cases that can occur.

447
00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:18.800
So external memory is much more difficult.

448
00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.300
Now this is particularly hard,

449
00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:28.080
it's not if the input is not only large,

450
00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:31.240
but if you make no assumptions about L1 and L2.

451
00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:35.920
Okay, so if my algorithm must work for arbitrary L1 and L2,

452
00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:38.380
this is a really, really difficult problem

453
00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:42.820
because arbitrary, unfortunately, includes many corner cases

454
00:28:42.820 --> 00:28:45.480
that you would think, oh, this will never occur in practice,

455
00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:47.400
but it can occur.

456
00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:48.740
Yeah, so we don't know this.

457
00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:53.200
And so, yeah, so there are many, many corner cases

458
00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:55.320
that we perhaps have to cover.

459
00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:59.100
Yeah, so for example, what never was the case

460
00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:01.120
for my example here,

461
00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:03.340
The list might contain duplicates.

462
00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:08.080
Now if it's a true duplicate in the sense that the elements are really identical, then

463
00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:09.900
it doesn't matter because we can just collapse them.

464
00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:16.180
But it could be that, or often will be the case, that it's a duplicate with attached additional

465
00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:19.600
information, which means you have a certain value and this has additional information

466
00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:24.760
that we must preserve, which means we cannot just remove duplicates, but we must keep them

467
00:29:24.760 --> 00:29:26.460
and we must output the duplicates correctly.

468
00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.620
So this occurs quite frequently.

469
00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:31.180
and then the duplicates

470
00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:31.800
create

471
00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:35.060
problems for us.

472
00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:36.920
So we must cope with this problem

473
00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:38.480
that we have duplicates and we must

474
00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:39.900
preserve them in the output.

475
00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:42.960
And now it could be very unlucky

476
00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:44.480
that we have so many duplicates

477
00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:46.860
that even a single duplicate exceeds my memory

478
00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:48.660
size. So I have this one

479
00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:50.780
value with attached extra

480
00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:52.680
information that is so

481
00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:54.480
frequent that

482
00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:56.520
I cannot store even

483
00:29:56.520 --> 00:29:58.540
all instances of this single value

484
00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:04.300
in memory. Now this breaks my simple external memory logics that just say,

485
00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:09.160
hey choose smaller partitionings and everything fits, because this never happens.

486
00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:13.640
So it's just impossible to partition the data so that it fits because of this

487
00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:19.840
duplicate problem. Now there are many ways to solve this. For example, you could

488
00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:24.460
