726
00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:57.080
So these are here, textbook.

727
00:46:57.400 --> 00:46:59.000
So these are textbook examples.

728
00:46:59.100 --> 00:47:01.080
You find them in books, but not in systems.

729
00:47:03.360 --> 00:47:06.920
The entries below this line, these are not textbooks, these are real.

730
00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:10.060
So these are used for implementing database systems.

731
00:47:12.140 --> 00:47:18.620
So semi-joints, we want to find all elements from the left side that do have a joint partner

732
00:47:18.620 --> 00:47:19.480
on the right hand side.

733
00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:23.000
So we look at elements from the left, all x from the left, and then we check if there

734
00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:25.120
exists an element on the right where the predicate is true.

735
00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:29.660
It doesn't matter if it is 1 or 20,000, we just care yes or no.

736
00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:31.020
Is there a joint partner, yes or no?

737
00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:33.500
And if yes, we output it, if no, we discard x.

738
00:47:34.480 --> 00:47:37.620
And then the anti-joint, this is a complement of the semi-joint.

739
00:47:37.620 --> 00:47:40.660
So we output everything where there is not a joint partner.

740
00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:44.920
Now you could ask yourself why do we need this.

741
00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:49.080
I can already tell you this year corresponds roughly to

742
00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:56.860
this year corresponds roughly to exists in SQL.

743
00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:09.100
exists as an existential quantifier and this here corresponds roughly to the price not exists.

744
00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:17.780
Okay, so we can use this to translate quantifier expression and sequel with semijoints and antitrions.

745
00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:21.460
And there are also some other use cases. And in fact life is difficult,

746
00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:31.020
For example, not exist with anti-joins works unless you can, for some strange reason, have

747
00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:33.220
null results, which unfortunately sometimes occur.

748
00:48:33.640 --> 00:48:39.120
But if you ignore this, then exist maps to semi-joins, not exist maps to anti-joins, except

749
00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:40.520
for some special cases.

750
00:48:42.980 --> 00:48:45.260
And then the next one here, outer-joins.

751
00:48:46.940 --> 00:48:59.060
So we output everything from, so this is basically a regular join followed by, so this here is a regular join followed by, we find all elements that did not find a joint partner.

752
00:48:59.140 --> 00:49:02.440
For this here we use anti-joint, we find all elements that do not have a joint partner.

753
00:49:02.680 --> 00:49:07.800
And then we output them regardless and we pad the missing values here with nulls.

754
00:49:07.800 --> 00:49:14.380
So we take a look at all the values from E2 which were missing, we had a null there and

755
00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:16.380
then we add them anyway.

756
00:49:17.380 --> 00:49:19.100
So this here is a left outer join.

757
00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:28.180
We output everything that either has a join partner or does not have a join partner and

758
00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:28.940
pad it with nulls.

759
00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:31.700
You get this for two reasons.

760
00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:46.600
either if the user asks for it, because in SQL you can write, so you can say table 1 or some other subquery here, then left autojoin

761
00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:59.060
table 2 on x equals y.

762
00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:00.980
This corresponds directly to an autogoint.

763
00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:03.820
So this is an explicit autogoint in the query.

764
00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:06.180
There are other ways you can get autogoints.

765
00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:08.140
For example, if somebody does, see this here,

766
00:50:08.560 --> 00:50:14.060
you say select, select, then some stuff,

767
00:50:14.240 --> 00:50:16.680
and then you do inside the select, you do another select here.

768
00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:26.000
select y from and some other.

769
00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:29.840
So this implicitly also creates an auto join.

770
00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:32.560
Because if this inner result here is empty,

771
00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:36.280
so if you do not find a y, then we get a null.

772
00:50:37.220 --> 00:50:39.880
And if you get more than one y, you get an error message.

773
00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:42.820
But nevertheless, this corresponds to a special kind of auto joins.

774
00:50:43.360 --> 00:50:52.740
So that we call them single join because you're only allowed to have one joint partner.

775
00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:54.800
But this corresponds roughly to an auto join.

776
00:50:56.060 --> 00:51:01.660
And finally, we have a full auto join that is in a way the combination of left and right

777
00:51:01.660 --> 00:51:02.200
auto join.

778
00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:06.860
Which means we take the regular join and then output everything from the left that does

779
00:51:06.860 --> 00:51:07.720
not have a joint partner.

780
00:51:07.900 --> 00:51:10.140
Then everything from the right that does not have a joint partner.

781
00:51:10.640 --> 00:51:15.900
Note that this here only holds the simple union because we use set semantics.

782
00:51:16.240 --> 00:51:21.240
With back semantics this does not work because we would include the regular join result twice.

783
00:51:21.780 --> 00:51:25.120
So this is a combination of left and right join only with sets.

784
00:51:25.280 --> 00:51:30.080
If you have duplicates, we must be more careful than we just take the regular join

785
00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:32.500
and add everything padded from left and from the right.

786
00:51:32.800 --> 00:51:36.360
Again, you can ask for this in SQL using full outer join.

787
00:51:39.320 --> 00:51:44.480
Now, up to now this part you have probably already seen in your introductory lecture.

788
00:51:44.740 --> 00:51:48.440
These are some operators that you might not have seen in introductory lecture, but we

789
00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:49.540
nevertheless need.

790
00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:54.180
So for real SQL support we need some more constructs.

791
00:51:54.340 --> 00:51:56.440
The first one here is a map operator.

792
00:51:57.180 --> 00:51:59.060
Basically we construct a new column.

793
00:51:59.220 --> 00:52:05.240
So we say we want to output the new column A and we want that for we construct the new

794
00:52:06.360 --> 00:52:08.580
by evaluating a function f on my input attributes.

795
00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:13.240
Now why do we need this? For example, it's very simple reasons.

796
00:52:13.680 --> 00:52:15.120
Somebody says, select

797
00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:23.120
x plus y as z.

798
00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:24.540
Okay? From r.

799
00:52:27.020 --> 00:52:30.700
So if you do this in SQL, if you translate this in relation algebra like this,

800
00:52:30.700 --> 00:52:41.040
I take my relation R, then I do a map and I say the column Z has the value X plus Y.

801
00:52:41.800 --> 00:52:48.400
So this is a way to compute a new column by evaluating a function.

802
00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:55.340
So this is a map function. Then we have a group by an aggregate. So I take my input,

803
00:52:55.340 --> 00:52:57.960
group it by a certain number of attributes,

804
00:52:58.160 --> 00:52:59.940
then evaluate a function f.

805
00:53:00.220 --> 00:53:02.000
This again corresponds to,

806
00:53:02.160 --> 00:53:04.840
so if I in SQL would do,

807
00:53:05.060 --> 00:53:05.900
select

808
00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:11.840
some

809
00:53:14.780 --> 00:53:15.460
x

810
00:53:16.180 --> 00:53:16.860
s

811
00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:17.480
s

812
00:53:18.260 --> 00:53:18.940
from

813
00:53:20.920 --> 00:53:21.600
r

814
00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:23.280
group by

815
00:53:26.980 --> 00:53:49.260
by ABC. This corresponds to the relation algebra construct. Take my relation R, then I add, on top of this, I add a group by. I group by ABC and then evaluate the aggregation function.

816
00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:58.280
So this is how we can translate group by and SQL into algebra using a group by operator.

817
00:53:59.660 --> 00:54:05.600
And finally dependent join, here we come back to the three variables that we had before.

818
00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:08.700
So this looks like a join but behaves slightly differently.

819
00:54:08.900 --> 00:54:14.340
Namely, what we do here is we have two expressions e1 and e2 and we evaluate the expression e1

820
00:54:14.340 --> 00:54:17.620
for the values from e2.

821
00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:26.060
We take an x from e1 and then we take a y from e2 evaluated for x.

822
00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:39.580
That works. To make an algebra example, we say I want to output, I have R1, then I do here a dependent join.

823
00:54:40.400 --> 00:54:52.840
and the dependent join says say r1.x equals r2.y then i have here in r2.

824
00:54:53.480 --> 00:55:01.100
Note that this part here is problematic because we try to evaluate predicate

825
00:55:01.100 --> 00:55:06.900
but using the values from r1 and r2 but at this point i do not have r1 available.

826
00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:10.620
So if you look at this part, so we ignore the rest.

827
00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:11.880
Let's look at this box.

828
00:55:12.460 --> 00:55:13.640
Just look at the box.

829
00:55:14.860 --> 00:55:17.260
This relational attribute operator is not well formed.

830
00:55:17.500 --> 00:55:19.860
Why? Because we are accessing attributes that does not exist.

831
00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:23.400
R1 doesn't exist, because within the box I only have R2,

832
00:55:23.620 --> 00:55:26.180
and I can evaluate R2.y, no problem,

833
00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:29.240
but I cannot evaluate R1.x,

834
00:55:29.900 --> 00:55:30.780
whereas this doesn't exist.

835
00:55:31.600 --> 00:55:44.840
So therefore we call r1.x, so the f of my box here is r1.x.

836
00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:47.940
So this is a free variable, which means I don't know it.

837
00:55:48.100 --> 00:55:49.380
So I have to bind it.

838
00:55:49.960 --> 00:55:52.020
And this is exactly what the dependent join does.

839
00:55:52.260 --> 00:55:56.480
The dependent join says within the right hand side,

840
00:55:56.620 --> 00:55:58.500
you please use the following value.

841
00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:03.360
namely we use my x, pass it into the right hand side and then we can evaluate this.

842
00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:07.060
So regular joint wouldn't work, but dependent joint does work.

843
00:56:08.000 --> 00:56:11.860
I just evaluate this box for the template from the left side.

844
00:56:12.500 --> 00:56:17.920
Now clearly this here has again n2 runtime.

845
00:56:18.480 --> 00:56:23.660
Why? Because I take a element from the left and then evaluate the right hand side for each element from the left.

846
00:56:23.780 --> 00:56:24.400
This is bad.

847
00:56:24.400 --> 00:56:28.820
Therefore, what the system will try to do is, it will try to remove all dependent joins.

848
00:56:29.140 --> 00:56:32.660
Dependent joins are performance-wise very, very bad. We don't want to do this.

849
00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:35.820
But there are SQL constructs that force us to do this.

850
00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:38.880
The most trivial example you can construct is this one here.

851
00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:47.760
You say select star from 1.

852
00:56:47.760 --> 00:57:06.020
Now we start a subquery, select star from R2 where R1.x equals R2.y.

853
00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:09.640
And if you have this, then you get exactly this construct.

854
00:57:10.140 --> 00:57:15.000
And you get exactly dependent join. Now the dependent join again are terrible,

855
00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:18.200
Therefore the first thing the system will do is to try to remove the dependent join.

856
00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:24.260
And you can hopefully easily see that you can transform this one here into

857
00:57:27.900 --> 00:57:28.860
1.join2

858
00:57:28.860 --> 00:57:29.980
on the attribute

859
00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:33.900
y.x equals 2.y.

860
00:57:34.600 --> 00:57:39.800
Okay, you can rewrite this so that the predicate moves up to the join, then it's no longer dependent

861
00:57:39.800 --> 00:57:42.380
and then we can use regular joins and everything is good.

862
00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:48.480
Okay, so in this simple case it's easy to do, but I can write much more complicated SQL statements.

863
00:57:48.560 --> 00:57:50.920
It's not that easy to remove the correlation.

864
00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:54.100
Therefore, systems do support dependent join.

865
00:57:56.460 --> 00:58:00.560
Now just for nomenclation, if you look at for example SQL server,

866
00:58:00.760 --> 00:58:05.740
SQL server doesn't call this dependent join, they call this I think cross apply.

867
00:58:13.720 --> 00:58:17.500
So cross apply, but nevertheless, this is the same as the dependent join.

868
00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:21.680
We evaluate the right-hand join side for the left-hand side.

869
00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:28.060
Now, why are we interested in set-oriented processing?

870
00:58:28.340 --> 00:58:30.280
Because we can implement this much more efficiently.

871
00:58:30.280 --> 00:58:36.800
So if you are processing not one value and one value, but if you are processing the million values and the million values,

872
00:58:36.940 --> 00:58:42.900
then we can perform operations that make the whole process more efficient.

873
00:58:43.000 --> 00:58:44.840
For example, we can build an index structure.

874
00:58:45.580 --> 00:58:48.160
Or we can reorganize the data like partitioning.

875
00:58:48.360 --> 00:58:50.340
Or we can use sorting or hashing.

876
00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:55.820
So we can do something with our data that we could not do if we just look at individual values.

877
00:58:56.000 --> 00:58:59.400
And this preprocessing makes the whole process more efficient.

878
00:59:01.740 --> 00:59:08.500
In the end, we want that all algorithms have a runtime of n log n or better, ideally.

879
00:59:09.740 --> 00:59:11.740
To be fair, this is not always possible.

880
00:59:12.040 --> 00:59:16.640
If the user asks me to produce an n-square output, I cannot be faster than n-square.

881
00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:22.980
But if the user does not force me to produce an n-square output, then I want to be faster

882
00:59:22.980 --> 00:59:23.560
than n-square.

883
00:59:23.800 --> 00:59:26.100
So n log n is what we want to have.

884
00:59:27.380 --> 00:59:31.720
And now there are many ways to achieve this, many algorithms we can use.

