these machines. But this finite number is much, much, much, much higher than a rotating disk.

961
01:03:54.660 --> 01:04:00.140
Yes, you can easily do thousands of random access per second, many thousands of random

962
01:04:00.140 --> 01:04:05.680
access per second, which you could never do on a rotating disk. So hardware has changed,

963
01:04:05.780 --> 01:04:12.060
therefore the old assumptions are not necessarily true. This has some consequences for the design

964
01:04:12.060 --> 01:04:18.160
of a database system. First, what we see is CPU costs suddenly become much more expensive,

965
01:04:18.320 --> 01:04:23.860
much more important. In the past, while we are waiting on disk, disk is so incredibly slow,

966
01:04:24.040 --> 01:04:28.780
that it doesn't matter that much what we do with the CPU. It does matter when we start burning

967
01:04:28.780 --> 01:04:33.500
CPU massively, but as long as we were reasonably with our CPU, then disk error was everything.

968
01:04:33.800 --> 01:04:38.220
This is no longer the case, because data is in memory to begin with, at least often,

969
01:04:38.220 --> 01:04:40.560
and then disk I.O. doesn't matter at all.

970
01:04:40.640 --> 01:04:42.020
We only look at CPU processing.

971
01:04:42.400 --> 01:04:43.620
So CPU becomes important.

972
01:04:44.440 --> 01:04:48.660
Because we often have no I.O. at all, or only very little.

973
01:04:49.140 --> 01:04:54.420
And then if you do I.O., we could potentially afford to do a bit more random I.O. than we did in the past.

974
01:04:54.540 --> 01:04:59.480
Because my I.O. device is much faster and it does allow for random access.

975
01:05:00.060 --> 01:05:04.700
And this means that the old architecture that people have used in the past has become suboptimal.

976
01:05:04.700 --> 01:05:10.380
So, you can run a traditional database architecture on a modern system.

977
01:05:10.480 --> 01:05:13.980
It will work, no problem, but it will not give you an ideal performance.

978
01:05:15.540 --> 01:05:18.260
Of course, we will look at the aspect of this in the lecture.

979
01:05:19.000 --> 01:05:22.160
But on the other hand, this is more an evolution than a revolution.

980
01:05:22.580 --> 01:05:26.060
So this doesn't mean you have to throw away all your database systems.

981
01:05:26.320 --> 01:05:28.240
It just means that you have to change something.

982
01:05:28.500 --> 01:05:33.400
You must change some of the techniques, but some of the things that we used in the past

983
01:05:34.760 --> 01:05:40.580
And therefore, we start by looking at the traditional architecture also because this

984
01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:44.220
is what you will mostly encounter if you look at the big commercial vendors.

985
01:05:44.380 --> 01:05:48.480
They still tend to be more designed like a traditional database system.

986
01:05:48.560 --> 01:05:49.480
Therefore, we start with this.

987
01:05:49.660 --> 01:05:57.100
And then we look at how we can adapt this to scale better to modern hardware design.

988
01:06:00.580 --> 01:06:05.620
The goals that you have in the database system is ideally we want the following.

989
01:06:05.800 --> 01:06:09.680
First we want to be able to handle arbitrary large amounts of data.

990
01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:11.700
If we want to do this efficiently.

991
01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:14.600
It doesn't matter if you have a gigabyte or if you have many terabytes.

992
01:06:15.460 --> 01:06:19.260
Everything should work smoothly and efficiently for arbitrary sizes.

993
01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:23.780
Now another goal is we never want to lose data.

994
01:06:23.780 --> 01:06:28.640
It doesn't matter what happens if we have a crash, software crash, power failure, whatever.

995
01:06:28.880 --> 01:06:29.920
We never want to lose data.

996
01:06:30.440 --> 01:06:32.460
This, of course, also requires care.

997
01:06:32.980 --> 01:06:37.280
Then we want to offer a high-level interface to manipulate and retrieve data.

998
01:06:37.480 --> 01:06:40.480
Usually, in our case, it's a SQL, but could also be something else.

999
01:06:40.660 --> 01:06:42.240
We also have some high-level API.

1000
01:06:42.920 --> 01:06:46.240
We want to shield the application from the complexity of data management,

1001
01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:48.980
which means that this application just says,

1002
01:06:49.080 --> 01:06:52.100
hey, here's my data, please store it, please retrieve it for me.

1003
01:06:53.780 --> 01:06:59.360
do this internally. Store it and prostrate it as it works best for my use case.

1004
01:06:59.720 --> 01:07:04.380
This is what we want, that the system does it automatically or at least mostly

1005
01:07:04.380 --> 01:07:09.220
automatically. And of course we want to get excellent performance for all kinds

1006
01:07:09.220 --> 01:07:13.340
of queries and all kinds of data. No assumptions about the data. Do you have

1007
01:07:13.340 --> 01:07:16.580
skew in the data, so uneven distribution? Do you have duplicates? Doesn't matter.

1008
01:07:16.720 --> 01:07:22.220
It should work in all cases. This is our goal and this is of course a very

1009
01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:27.600
satisfy all these goals. On the other hand, we usually can do this

1010
01:07:27.600 --> 01:07:32.060
just with added complexity in the software. But that's okay, because this is a deal

1011
01:07:32.060 --> 01:07:35.520
that we made when we built a database system. Database systems are

1012
01:07:36.100 --> 01:07:39.000
pretty difficult to build, but you build them only once

1013
01:07:39.000 --> 01:07:42.900
or a few times. There are not that many database systems

1014
01:07:42.900 --> 01:07:46.680
out there. Actually, there are many database systems, if you are honest.

1015
01:07:47.320 --> 01:07:51.100
But nevertheless, there are even more applications out there

1016
01:07:51.100 --> 01:07:51.960
than database systems.

1017
01:07:52.260 --> 01:07:56.360
So usually one database system can be used by many different kinds of applications.

1018
01:07:56.840 --> 01:08:02.260
Therefore, it's just worthwhile to spend the implementation effort and do it once, and then

1019
01:08:02.260 --> 01:08:03.540
everybody can profit from this.

1020
01:08:06.420 --> 01:08:11.780
So then this here is a rough overview of what we want to do in the lecture.

1021
01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:16.840
So we start with the classic architecture, which means we look at how traditional database

1022
01:08:16.840 --> 01:08:18.340
systems are organized.

1023
01:08:19.340 --> 01:08:26.000
We start with storage. So how do we store data on disk or on a physical medium?

1024
01:08:26.560 --> 01:08:32.220
Then we look at access paths. If you don't know what access paths means, first

1025
01:08:32.220 --> 01:08:36.660
approximation, an access path is an index structure. So how can we access my data

1026
01:08:36.660 --> 01:08:40.260
efficiently? How can we find my data efficiently? Then we look at transactions

1027
01:08:40.260 --> 01:08:44.220
and recovery. So how can we cope with concurrent users and how can we cope with

1028
01:08:44.220 --> 01:08:50.660
crashes. This covers the first big part of the lecture. Next big part is

1029
01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:57.880
efficient query processing. So how do we set oriented processing? How do we

1030
01:08:57.880 --> 01:09:01.820
implement this efficiently? How can we pass data around and so on? Then we look at

1031
01:09:01.820 --> 01:09:05.860
the algebraic operators. How can we implement all of them efficiently? So

1032
01:09:05.860 --> 01:09:10.980
that we can get fast answers for all these primitives. And third part here is

1033
01:09:10.980 --> 01:09:14.680
code generation. If we have a query, how do we translate this into something

1034
01:09:14.680 --> 01:09:19.200
executable? Because ultimately we must execute a query. Because we want to get

1035
01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:25.800
results, we must execute the query. How do we do this? And in the third part,

1036
01:09:26.020 --> 01:09:31.060
designing a database system for the modern hardware. So what do we

1037
01:09:31.060 --> 01:09:34.600
have to change in the storage architectures to cope with, for example,

1038
01:09:34.600 --> 01:09:42.880
large main memory. Then how do you optimize cache locality and finally if you are building a main

1039
01:09:42.880 --> 01:09:47.580
memory database system so that assumes that all my data is memory nearly all the time what we have

1040
01:09:47.580 --> 01:09:52.260
to do there because this affects not just storage but many other parts like transaction processing

1041
01:09:52.260 --> 01:09:59.180
and recovery and so on. And this is a brief overview over the lecture. Okay so I will stop here with

1042
